_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_

**Validate that `refs[refId].url` exists.**

The check at line 242-244 throws an error if `refs[refId]` is falsy, but after this check, line 251 directly accesses `refs[refId].url` without verifying it exists. If a ref object is present but missing the `url` property, the result would be `"undefined/iframe.html"`.



<details>
<summary>🔎 Proposed fix to validate url property</summary>

```diff
   if (refId && !refs[refId]) {
     throw new Error(`Invalid refId: ${refId}`);
   }
+  if (refId && !refs[refId].url) {
+    throw new Error(`Ref ${refId} does not have a url`);
+  }

   const originAddress = global.window.location.origin + location.pathname;
   const networkAddress = global.STORYBOOK_NETWORK_ADDRESS ?? originAddress;
   const managerBase =
     base === 'origin' ? originAddress : base === 'network' ? networkAddress : location.pathname;
   const previewBase = refId
     ? refs[refId].url + '/iframe.html'
     : global.PREVIEW_URL || `${managerBase}iframe.html`;
```

</details>

<details>
<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>

```
In code/core/src/manager-api/modules/url.ts around lines 242 to 252, after
confirming refs[refId] exists you must also validate that refs[refId].url is a
non-empty string before using it; update the logic so if refId is provided but
refs[refId].url is missing/invalid you either throw a clear error (e.g. `Invalid
refId url: ${refId}`) or fall back to the existing
global.PREVIEW_URL/managerBase iframe path — ensure you do not construct
`${refs[refId].url}/iframe.html` when url is undefined.
```

</details>

